#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186298845 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "039f9f0b-70dd-5360-9240-2260ff706c93" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Coefficient in a Second-Degree Taylor Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a1a8b306-86e6-5d55-be87-1e3917b8a5d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298847} {:answer/id #uuid "7e84e586-4594-557e-8a58-7bb94ad7af1d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298848} {:answer/id #uuid "e36dd7bb-8f18-5419-96b1-0abf0db3034e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298849} {:answer/id #uuid "35dc4de5-8c28-558c-a14f-6835fc292143" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298850} {:answer/id #uuid "0b56f66e-39cc-5d76-b8ee-98cffe2ba845" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298851}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a1a8b306-86e6-5d55-be87-1e3917b8a5d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298847} :answer-field/id #uuid "7242b31d-06a4-5a77-bbf0-3eae604e6c07" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298846}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a000ea29-976d-55b5-925a-b906444d88a0" :question/math-academy-id "q-81059" :question/problem "What is the coefficient of ${(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{2}$ in the second-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}?$" :question/worked-solution "The second-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)$ about $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{2}(x) & =f(\\frac{\\pi }{4})+{f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})+\\frac{1}{2}{f}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}){(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe coefficient of ${(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{2}$ in the above Taylor polynomial is \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{f}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}).\n$$\n\nTo find this coefficient, we start by computing the second derivative ${f}^{″}(x):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we evaluate ${f}^{″}(x)$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ This gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we determine the coefficient of ${(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{f}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
